 ## Overview
 
-A Signed Distance Field (SDF) Primitive Definition is an analytical function that returns the shortest Euclidean distance from a query point $\mathbf{p}$ in 3D space to the surface of a geometric primitive, with a sign indicating whether the point is inside (negative) or outside (positive) the shape [S2, S5, S6, S12, unofficial]. It solves the problem of representing complex 3D geometry as a continuous scalar field, replacing explicit mesh topology with implicit distance evaluations [S4, unofficial]. Unlike sibling [Constructive SDF Modeling (Min Combination)](871-constructive-sdf-modeling-min-combination.md) which composites primitives, or [SDF Transformation](1086-sdf-transformation.md) which modifies the input coordinate space, this method defines the fundamental geometric primitives themselves [G]. It is distinguished from mesh-based rendering by allowing complex scenes to be constructed via simple arithmetic on scalar values, providing a conservative distance estimate that guarantees no surface is closer than $d(\mathbf{p})$, preventing ray overshooting [S6, S12, unofficial].
+A Signed Distance Field (SDF) Primitive Definition is an analytical function that returns the shortest Euclidean distance from a query point $\mathbf{p}$ in 3D space to the surface of a geometric primitive, with a sign indicating whether the point is inside (negative) or outside (positive) the shape [S2, unofficial][S5, unofficial][S6, unofficial][S12, unofficial]. It solves the problem of representing complex 3D geometry as a continuous scalar field, replacing explicit mesh topology with implicit distance evaluations [S4, unofficial]. Unlike sibling [Constructive SDF Modeling (Min Combination)](871-constructive-sdf-modeling-min-combination.md) which composites primitives, or [SDF Transformation](1086-sdf-transformation.md) which modifies the input coordinate space, this method defines the fundamental geometric primitives themselves [G]. It is distinguished from mesh-based rendering by allowing complex scenes to be constructed via simple arithmetic on scalar values, providing a conservative distance estimate that guarantees no surface is closer than $d(\mathbf{p})$, preventing ray overshooting [S6, unofficial][S12, unofficial].
 
